On 2 Aug 2014, Ray Chason wrote:
> YANI (and I'm just blowing smoke, since I'm not in charge of NAO):
> Known griefers get a secret "griefer" flag set on their accounts.  Bones
> saved from a griefer account are then visible only to other griefers.

You don't need to resort to manual identification of griefer accounts.

Griefers, particularly artless grab-the-biggest-hammer types like the one
that started this discussion, use moves that someone playing to win is
very unlikely to use.  So nethack could be modified to store a "tilt
flag" when the user does something suspicious, such as reverse genociding
powerful monsters.  If the character dies while tilt is set, no bones
will ever be dropped.

It does mean you'll lose "honest" bones where someone picks up a scroll of
genocide, gets attacked by a polytrap-generated arch-lich 10 moves later,
and decides to take the risk of YASD from reading a scroll of unknown
beatitude over the certainty of YAAD from fighting an early arch-lich
straight. But I'd imagine most people could live with that.

Note that this could be made more effective by not disclosing precisely
what triggers the tilt flag.  Since most bones-stuffing attempts will
fail to take anyway, it will be hard for the griefer to figure out
whether he's doing something wrong or whether he is just unlucky....

Tilt flags could also be used to supress users who try to stuff
unnaturally *helpful* bones, for the purposes of easier multiconduct
ascensions.

